---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import Callout from '../../components/Callout.astro';

const title = 'Create a Land Trust Online: How Remote Real Estate Privacy Works | FreeTrustDocs';
const description = 'Can you create a land trust online? Yes for the paperwork. Here is what a land trust agreement needs, which states recognize them, and what still happens offline.';
---

<Base title={title} description={description} ogType="article">
  <section class="section">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="breadcrumbs">
        <a href="/">Home</a> <span>›</span> <a href="/guide/">Guides</a> <span>›</span> Land Trusts Online
      </div>
      <h1>Create a Land Trust Online: How Remote Real Estate Privacy Works</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        You can build a land trust from your kitchen table. The trust agreement is an ordinary private
        document, and drafting it happens in your browser. But parts of a land trust are, and always will
        be, offline acts. A notarized deed, a county recording, a bank account. Here's the honest line
        between the two.
      </p>

      <h2>What actually happens online</h2>
      <p>
        Three things, and they're the drafting work:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>The <strong>trust agreement</strong> names the trustee, defines the beneficiary's powers, and
        sets succession. This is the whole legal engine of a land trust, and it's drafted online.</li>
        <li>The <strong>assignment of beneficial interest</strong> moves the economic interest, signed
        separately from the deed. Also drafted online.</li>
        <li>Most of the <strong>coordination</strong>: choosing the trustee (an LLC works, and so can a
        friend), preparing the deed, filling out IRS Form SS-4 for the trust's EIN.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        Need the agreement itself? FreeTrustDocs generates a complete
        <a href="/guide/land-trust-agreement/">land trust agreement</a> in your browser, no signup, nothing
        uploaded.
      </p>

      <h2>What still happens offline</h2>
      <p>
        The county clerk has not gone digital, and the notary hasn't either:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>The deed must be notarized.</strong> Whatever instrument conveys the property into the
        trust, usually a quitclaim deed, needs a notary's seal before the county will record it. Online
        notarization is accepted in a growing number of states. Where it isn't, that's a sidewalk trip.</li>
        <li><strong>Recording happens at the county.</strong> The recorded deed is what puts the trustee's
        name on public record. That part is physical paperwork with a filing fee.</li>
        <li><strong>Title insurance and lenders.</strong> If there's a mortgage, the lender gets a say
        before any transfer, and a title company will review the land trust arrangement during a sale or
        refinance.</li>
      </ul>
      <Callout variant="info">
        The deed to real property always needs notarization. This is separate from the trust instrument
        itself, which in most states needs only the settlor's signature.
        <a href="/guide/trust-notarization/">Full notarization guide →</a>
      </Callout>

      <h2>Which states recognize land trusts</h2>
      <p>
        Land trust case law is strongest in Illinois, and only a handful of states have explicit land trust
        statutes. Florida's Land Trust Act (§689.071) is the most cited. Court-recognized practice covers
        several more, including Indiana, North Dakota, Virginia, Ohio, and Georgia. In states that don't
        recognize them, and California and Texas are the big ones, an LLC is the standard privacy tool
        instead.
      </p>
      <p>
        Check your state before you draft. The
        <a href="/land-trust/">land trust state index</a> lists recognizing states, and the
        <a href="/land-trust/non-recognizing-states/">non-recognizing states</a> page covers the LLC
        alternative.
      </p>

      <h2>Privacy: what a land trust does and doesn't hide</h2>
      <p>
        A land trust keeps the beneficial owner's name off deeds, tax rolls, and county websites. It does
        not make you invisible. The beneficiary signs the deed transferring property into the trust, so the
        grantor's name appears on that one recorded document. And beneficial interests in a revocable land
        trust are reachable by a court. Consider it privacy from casual lookup, not protection from a
        subpoena.
      </p>
      <p>
        Which, honestly, is the right frame for most owners. My own rule: if what you need is protection
        from a known adversary with a lawyer, that's a conversation for an attorney, not a form. A land
        trust buys you quiet on the public record. It doesn't buy you a defense.
      </p>

      <h2>Costs, briefly</h2>
      <p>
        The drafts are free here. The unavoidable out-of-pocket items are county recording fees, the
        notary's fee, and, if you use one, the annual registered-agent or LLC fee for the trustee. A typical
        recording runs somewhere between $25 and $100 depending on the county. The EIN application is free
        directly through the IRS.
      </p>

      <h2>The sequence</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Confirm your state recognizes land trusts (or pick the LLC route).</li>
        <li>Generate the trust agreement and assignment online. Pick a trustee who isn't you.</li>
        <li>Get the EIN from the IRS under the trust's name.</li>
        <li>Sign the deed before a notary and record it at the county.</li>
        <li>Update the homeowner's insurance policy to name the trust.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        Step 4 is where most DIY attempts stall. It's an offline step no website can do for you.
      </p>

      <Callout variant="warning" label="Not legal advice">
        General information as of 2026, not legal advice. State rules vary on transfer taxes, due-on-sale
        risk, and homestead exemptions. If the property has a mortgage or the transfer is anything but
        simple, run the deed past a local real estate attorney.
      </Callout>
    </div>
  </section>
</Base>